 {"success": true, "name": "continual-arc", "file": "references/noisy-object-recovery.md", "content": "# Noisy object recovery after incorrect predictions\n\nWhen an ARC demo replaces a noisy scene with cleaned geometric objects, first compute the exact input/output diff and classify each changed cell by its original color and location. In multiple examples, test whether unrelated singleton noise is deleted while regular shapes remain, whether intrusions inside shapes are repaired, and whether holes are filled.\n\n## Avoiding overgeneralization\n\n- A visual resemblance to a rectangle is not sufficient evidence to complete its bounding box. Confirm the object's footprint, color, dimensions, and location are supported by the demos.\n- Check whether some shapes are intentionally partial, clipped, or made of multiple disconnected fragments. Derive the mask rule, not an aesthetic cleanup rule.\n- Enumerate every color/component and distinguish object pixels from distractor markers; avoid assuming a color is noise based on one scene.\n- After a wrong submission, reject the whole hypothesis. Revisit trigger and output-mask questions, and use another demo if the surviving hypotheses predict different outputs.\n- Mechanically validate every changed cell and ensure the proposed transformation explains unchanged cells as well as edits.\n\nThis complements `noisy-multi-object-denoising.md`: that reference describes object inventory and reconstruction, while this note emphasizes restraint after failed guesses and exact diff analysis.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/c
